        "content": "A weed is defined agronomically as any plant growing where it is not desired, possessing traits that enable it to colonize disturbed agro-ecosystems and compete aggressively with cultivated crops for light, nutrients, moisture, and physical space.\n\n### Botanical and Ecological Classification of Weeds\n- **By Morphology & Cotyledon Number:**\n  - *Grasses (Monocots - Poaceae):* Hollow cylindrical stems with nodes and internodes, parallel-veined narrow leaves with open sheaths, fibrous roots (e.g., *Echinochloa colona*, *Eleusine indica*, *Cynodon dactylon*).\n  - *Sedges (Monocots - Cyperaceae):* Solid triangular stems without nodes, 3-ranked leaves with closed sheaths, modified rhizomes/tubers (e.g., *Cyperus rotundus*, *Cyperus iria*).\n  - *Broad-leaved Weeds (Dicots):* Expanded reticulate-veined leaves, net venation, taproot system (e.g., *Parthenium hysterophorus*, *Amaranthus viridis*, *Trianthema portulacastrum*, *Commelina benghalensis*).\n- **By Life Span:** Annuals (complete lifecycle within one season; *Echinochloa crus-galli*), Biennials (*Daucus carota* wild), and Perennials (propagate through vegetative tubers/stolons and seeds; *Cyperus rotundus*, *Sorghum halepense*).\n- **By Parasitism:** Semi-root parasite (*Striga asiatica* in sorghum/maize), Complete root parasite (*Orobanche* spp.), Semi-stem parasite (*Loranthus*), and Complete stem parasite (*Cuscuta* spp.).\n\n### Critical Period of Weed Competition (CPWC)\nThe CPWC is the critical window in the crop growth cycle during which weeds must be controlled to prevent unrecoverable economic yield losses exceeding an acceptable threshold (typically 5%). Weeds emerging prior to or after this window cause negligible yield loss.\n- **Direct Seeded Rice (DSR):** 15\u201345 DAS (yield loss up to 80\u2013100% if unweeded).\n- **Transplanted Puddled Rice:** 20\u201340 DAT.\n- **Maize:** 15\u201335 DAS.\n- **Soybean / Groundnut:** 15\u201330 DAS.\n- **Cotton:** 15\u201360 DAS.",
