        "id": "sec-agp218-5-2",
        "heading": "2. Herbicide Classification, Modes of Action, and Application",
        "content": "Herbicides are chemical compounds that kill or severely inhibit plant growth through targeted interference with specific biochemical and metabolic enzymes.\n\n### Classification by Time of Application\n- **Pre-Plant Incorporation (PPI):** Applied and mechanically incorporated into soil prior to sowing to prevent volatility or photodecomposition (e.g., Fluchloralin @ 1.0 kg a.i./ha, Trifluralin).\n- **Pre-Emergence (PRE):** Applied onto the soil surface after crop sowing but before crop and weed seedling emergence (within 0\u20133 DAS) (e.g., Pendimethalin 30% EC, Atrazine 50% WP, Pretilachlor 50% EC with safener).\n- **Post-Emergence (POST):** Applied directly onto foliage after crop and weeds have emerged (typically 15\u201325 DAS) (e.g., Imazethapyr 10% SL, Bispyribac-sodium 10% SC, 2,4-D amine salt).\n\n### Major Herbicide Biochemical Modes of Action\n1. **ALS (Acetolactate Synthase) Inhibitors (Group 2):** Inhibit synthesis of branched-chain amino acids (valine, leucine, isoleucine); e.g., Sulfonylureas (Pyrazosulfuron-ethyl, Halosulfuron) and Imidazolinones (Imazethapyr).\n2. **ACCase (Acetyl-CoA Carboxylase) Inhibitors (Group 1):** Inhibit fatty acid biosynthesis in grasses exclusively; e.g., Fenoxaprop-p-ethyl, Cyhalofop-butyl.\n3. **Photosystem-II (PS-II) Inhibitors (Group 5 & 6):** Block electron transport at the plastoquinone-B binding site of the D1 protein in photosystem II; e.g., Triazines (Atrazine in maize/sugarcane).\n4. **Microtubule Assembly Inhibitors (Group 3):** Bind tubulin, arresting spindle fiber formation and mitotic cell division in emerging radicles; e.g., Dinitroanilines (Pendimethalin, Trifluralin).\n5. **Synthetic Auxins (Group 4):** Mimic indole-3-acetic acid (IAA), disrupting phloem transport and cell wall plasticity in dicots; e.g., 2,4-D, MCPA.",
