      "questionText": "Define the Critical Period of Weed Competition (CPWC) and explain its practical significance in weed management.",
      "modelAnswer": "The Critical Period of Weed Competition (CPWC) is defined as the specific, critical window in the crop growth cycle during which weeds must be controlled to prevent unrecoverable economic yield losses exceeding an acceptable threshold (typically 5%). It is bounded by two distinct biological parameters:\n\n1. **Beginning of the CPWC (Critical Weed-Free Period):** The maximum length of time after crop emergence that weeds can be allowed to grow alongside the crop before their competitive interference causes >5% yield loss.\n2. **End of the CPWC (Critical Duration of Weed Competition):** The minimum length of time the crop canopy must be kept free of weeds so that later-emerging weeds fail to compete effectively with the closed canopy or cause economic yield loss.\n\n### Practical Significance in Agronomy\n- **Cost & Resource Optimization:** Eliminates the mistaken belief that fields must be kept 100% weed-free season-long. Weeds emerging after the CPWC (e.g., after 35 DAS in maize) cause negligible yield loss.\n- **Targeted Chemical Use:** Concentrates herbicide applications or intercultural weeding strictly within this window (e.g., 15\u201335 DAS), cutting pesticide costs, avoiding chemical toxicity, and preventing unnecessary labor expenses.",
