      "questionText": "Classify herbicides based on their application timing with suitable examples for each category.",
      "modelAnswer": "Herbicides are systematically classified into three distinct categories based on their chronological time of application relative to crop sowing and emergence:\n\n1. **Pre-Plant Incorporation (PPI):**\n   - Applied onto the field and mechanically incorporated into the top 3\u20135 cm of soil prior to sowing the crop.\n   - *Rationale:* Essential for volatile or photo-degradable chemical compounds.\n   - *Examples:* **Fluchloralin** (Basalin) @ 1.0 kg a.i./ha in soybean, groundnut, and cotton; **Trifluralin**.\n2. **Pre-Emergence (PRE):**\n   - Applied onto the soil surface after crop sowing but strictly before the emergence of crop seedlings and weed seeds (typically 0 to 3 DAS).\n   - *Rationale:* Creates a lethal chemical barrier in the top soil layer; emerging weed radicles and plumules absorb the herbicide during germination.\n   - *Examples:* **Pendimethalin 30% EC** @ 1.0 kg a.i./ha in pulses, cotton, and soybean; **Atrazine 50% WP** @ 1.0 kg a.i./ha in maize; **Pretilachlor** in transplanted rice.\n3. **Post-Emergence (POST):**\n   - Applied directly onto the green foliage after both the crop and weeds have successfully emerged from the soil (typically 15 to 25 DAS).\n   - *Rationale:* Selectively absorbed by weed foliage, translocating to disrupt active enzyme systems.\n   - *Examples:* **Imazethapyr 10% SL** @ 100 g a.i./ha in soybean/groundnut; **Bispyribac-sodium 10% SC** @ 25 g a.i./ha in rice; **Fenoxaprop-p-ethyl** in pulses; **2,4-D** in wheat and sugarcane.",
