      "questionText": "Explain the biochemical mode of action of Acetolactate Synthase (ALS) inhibitor herbicides.",
      "modelAnswer": "Acetolactate Synthase (ALS) inhibitors\u2014encompassing the Sulfonylureas (e.g., Pyrazosulfuron-ethyl, Metsulfuron-methyl) and Imidazolinones (e.g., Imazethapyr)\u2014are widely used systemic herbicides.\n\n### Biochemical Mode of Action\n1. **Target Enzyme Inhibition:** These herbicides bind with high affinity to the catalytic domain of the enzyme **Acetolactate Synthase (ALS)**, also known as **Acetohydroxyacid Synthase (AHAS)**, located within chloroplasts.\n2. **Disruption of Amino Acid Biosynthesis:** ALS catalyzes the initial common biochemical step in the synthesis of the three essential **branched-chain amino acids**: valine, leucine, and isoleucine. Specifically, it condenses two pyruvate molecules to form 2-acetolactate (pathway to valine/leucine) or condenses pyruvate and 2-ketobutyrate to form 2-aceto-2-hydroxybutyrate (pathway to isoleucine).\n3. **Physiological Consequences:** Inhibition of ALS triggers an acute starvation of branched-chain amino acids inside dividing cells. Protein synthesis ceases, causing rapid cessation of cell division in root and shoot growing tips within hours. Toxic accumulation of alpha-ketobutyrate further damages cellular metabolism. Sensitive weeds display interveinal chlorosis, purpling of veins, meristematic necrosis, and complete plant death within 7 to 14 days.",
