      "modelAnswer": "The **Stale Seedbed Technique** is a non-chemical cultural weed management practice implemented prior to crop sowing to exhaust the viable weed seed bank in the topsoil layer.\n\n### Operational Procedure\n1. **Seedbed Preparation & Pre-Sowing Irrigation:** The field is plowed, harrowed, and leveled into a final seedbed 2 to 3 weeks before the scheduled crop sowing date. A light irrigation (or relying on early pre-monsoon showers) is provided.\n2. **Stimulation of Weed Flushes:** Soil moisture and aeration induce the dormant seeds of annual weeds residing in the top 2\u20133 cm soil layer to germinate in a uniform, dense flush within 7 to 10 days.\n3. **Non-Disturbance Destruction:** Once weed seedlings reach the 2- to 3-leaf stage, they are eliminated without disturbing deeper soil layers. This is accomplished either mechanically using very shallow surface harrowing (spike tooth harrow or sweep cultivator operating at <2 cm depth) or chemically by spraying a non-selective, non-residual knockdown herbicide (e.g., **Paraquat @ 0.5 kg/ha** or **Glyphosate @ 1.0 kg/ha**).\n4. **Crop Sowing:** The crop is sown with minimal soil disturbance using zero-till seed drills.\n\n### Agronomic Benefits\nReduces emerging weed pressure in the standing crop by 50\u201370%, provides crop seedlings with an early competitive head-start, and reduces the need for subsequent post-emergence herbicide applications.",
