      "syllabusTopic": "Crop rotation, sequential cropping systems, and conservation agriculture (zero tillage and residue management)",
      "questionText": "State four fundamental agronomic principles that govern the design of sustainable crop rotations.",
      "modelAnswer": "A sustainable crop rotation involves scheduled alternation of different botanical families and plant architectures on the same field over successive seasons. It is governed by four core principles:\n\n1. **Alternation of Deep-Rooted and Shallow-Rooted Crops:** Deep-rooted taproot crops (e.g., Pigeonpea, Cotton) should be rotated with shallow fibrous-rooted crops (e.g., Wheat, Rice). This extracts plant nutrients and water differentially across vertical soil horizons and prevents plow pan formation.\n2. **Alternation of Exhaustive and Restorative Crops:** Fertility-exhaustive crops that consume heavy nitrogen and potassium (e.g., Maize, Sugarcane, Sorghum) should be followed by restorative grain or green manure legumes (e.g., Green gram, Cowpea, Chickpea) that fix atmospheric nitrogen and add organic leaf biomass.\n3. **Alternation of Botanical Families to Break Pest Cycles:** Rotating across unrelated plant families (e.g., Poaceae rotated with Fabaceae or Brassicaceae) breaks host-specific life cycles of soil-borne fungi, parasitic nematodes, and insect pests.\n4. **Alternation of Weed-Vulnerable and Smother Crops:** Slow-growing crops susceptible to weed choking (e.g., Onion, Direct Seeded Rice) should be rotated with dense, fast-canopy smother crops (e.g., Cowpea, Soybean) that suppress weed proliferation.",
