      "syllabusTopic": "Crop rotation, sequential cropping systems, and conservation agriculture (zero tillage and residue management)",
      "questionText": "Evaluate the Rice-Wheat cropping system in the Indo-Gangetic Plains, detailing its sustainability challenges and agronomic diversification pathways.",
      "modelAnswer": "The Rice\u2013Wheat system (RWS) covers roughly 13.5 million hectares across the Indo-Gangetic Plains (IGP) of India, Pakistan, Nepal, and Bangladesh. In India (Punjab, Haryana, UP, Bihar), it generates over 75% of national food grain reserves, serving as the backbone of national food security.\n\n### 1. Second-Generation Sustainability Crises in RWS\nAfter four decades of continuous intensive mono-cropping, the system is facing severe ecological fatigue:\n- **Groundwater Depletion:** Lowland puddled transplanted rice consumes 3,000 to 5,000 liters of water per kg of grain. In northwest India, tubewell over-extraction has caused water tables to plunge at 0.5 to 1.0 m/year, exhausting deep aquifers.\n- **Soil Hardpan & Structural Degradation:** Repetitive wet puddling destroys soil macro-aggregates, dispersing clay particles to form a dense, impenetrable hardpan at 15\u201320 cm depth. While this pan reduces percolation in rice, it severely restricts deep root penetration and oxygen aeration for succeeding winter wheat.\n- **Residue Burning & Air Quality Crises:** In mechanized northwest India, the brief 15\u201320 day window between rice harvest and wheat sowing prompts farmers to burn roughly 20 million tonnes of rice straw annually, releasing toxic particulate matter (PM2.5), $CO_2$, and nitrous oxides.\n- **Herbicide Resistance & Pest Shifts:** Continuous reliance on isoproturon and clodinafop has produced multiple-herbicide resistant *Phalaris minor*, while un-aerated paddies emit high methane ($CH_4$).\n\n### 2. Agronomic Diversification and Sustainable Transitions\n- **Adoption of Direct Seeded Rice (DSR) & Happy Seeder Wheat:** Transitioning to DSR avoids puddling and cuts irrigation water use by 25\u201330%. Sowing wheat with the Turbo Happy Seeder into standing rice residue eliminates stubble burning while conserving moisture.\n- **Crop Diversification (Replacing Kharif Rice):** Divert 20\u201330% of kharif rice acreage to lower-water-demand cash crops: **Hybrid Maize**, **Soybean**, or **Cotton**. Maize requires only one-fifth the water of rice and vacates fields early for winter crops.\n- **Inclusion of Short-Duration Catch Crops:** Introduce a 60-day summer pulse\u2014**Summer Mungbean (*Vigna radiata*)**\u2014between wheat harvest and rice sowing. This generates 1.0 t/ha of protein-rich grain, fixes 40 kg N/ha, and restores soil organic matter.\n- **Paira / Utera Cropping in Eastern IGP:** In eastern flood-prone tracts, relay sow lathyrus or lentil into standing paddy 10 days before harvest to utilize residual soil moisture without tillage costs.",
