      "difficulty": "Medium",
      "syllabusTopic": "Herbicide classification (chemical families, modes of action, selectivity, and application timing)",
      "questionText": "Compare Contact Herbicides versus Systemic (Translocated) Herbicides across translocation, coverage requirements, control of perennials, and speed of action.",
      "modelAnswer": "Herbicides differ fundamentally in their mobility within plant vascular tissues and their mode of plant destruction:\n\n1. **Vascular Translocation:**\n   - *Contact Herbicides:* Exhibit negligible mobility within the plant. They kill only the plant tissues directly contacted by spray droplets, failing to move into untargeted leaves or root systems.\n   - *Systemic Herbicides:* Readily absorbed by roots or foliage and translocate throughout the entire plant system via xylem (apoplastic) and/or phloem (symplastic) transport mechanisms to active meristems.\n2. **Spray Coverage Requirements:**\n   - *Contact Herbicides:* Require complete, dense spray droplet coverage (high spray volumes: 400\u2013600 L/ha) with fine droplets to ensure thorough wetting of all foliage. Unsprayed leaves remain alive.\n   - *Systemic Herbicides:* Tolerate moderate droplet coverage (lower spray volumes: 150\u2013250 L/ha); absorbed molecules travel to distant un-sprayed vegetative and root tissues.\n3. **Control of Perennial Weeds:**\n   - *Contact Herbicides:* Ineffective against perennial weeds (*Cyperus*, *Cynodon*, *Sorghum halepense*); they burn off above-ground foliage, but subterranean rhizomes and tubers quickly regenerate fresh shoots.\n   - *Systemic Herbicides:* Highly effective against perennials; systemic molecules translocate basipetally into subterranean tubers and rhizomes, destroying vegetative buds.\n4. **Speed of Action and Symptomology:**\n   - *Contact Herbicides:* Extremely rapid; cell membrane rupture leads to wilting and desiccation within 2 to 24 hours.\n   - *Systemic Herbicides:* Slower action; systemic enzyme inhibition and meristem necrosis require 7 to 21 days for complete plant mortality.\n5. **Representative Examples:**\n   - *Contact:* Paraquat, Diquat, Glufosinate-ammonium.\n   - *Systemic:* Glyphosate, Imazethapyr, 2,4-D, Atrazine.",
      "outlinePoints": [
        "Vascular mobility: localized tissue kill vs xylem/phloem systemic translocation",
        "Spray droplet coverage: high-volume dense coverage vs moderate-volume systemic uptake",
        "Efficacy on perennials: quick top-kill with rapid resprouting vs deep subterranean rhizome destruction",
        "Speed of symptom expression: rapid (2-24 hours) vs gradual (7-21 days)",
        "Commercial examples: Paraquat/Diquat (contact) vs Glyphosate/Imazethapyr (systemic)"
      ]
